so tell me where the idea for  interceptor came from well interceptor  began  it was an idea i wanted to write because  i wanted to move from novels into  directing movies but i had to come up  with something that would be  thrilling and fast but also you know  make makeable at a certain budget so i  was going to write this story set in  like an alaskan cia listening post  and this this cia agent was going to  hear you know  missiles being fired and had a direct  line to the white house  and as i prepared that i discovered  sbx1 and fought greeley and the  interceptor missile program  so  and i found that that was far more  interesting and so once i set the story  on spx-1 i had my gripping action  thriller  especially when you discover that it  really does take  24 minutes for a nuclear missile to fly  from russia to the us and you have 12  minutes to stop it as an action thriller  writer i i had my story then the timing  of it it's remarkably well timed did you  was that just luck of the draw was it  held or anything like that  uh no no it was purely coincidental i  wrote interceptor in 2017  uh and it went through the vicissitudes  of hollywood uh and no we it got the  it got the green light  in late 2020 we filmed it in 21 so yeah  um  purely coincidental that it's it's  becoming rather frighteningly applicable  now you said you filmed in 2021 did you  have a lot of trouble with the covid  process  we filmed in in sydney australia and we  that was where we were just  totally lucky we filmed in that middle  period where sydney was was open  uh and very very safe  we did have covered protocols on the set  um but no we got very lucky we filmed i  was about three quarters of the way  through the edit when sydney went into  lockdown again  so  we  we were just fortunate that is that is  blind luck  um what is the difference in your  process between writing a novel and  writing a screenplay a novel is like  running a marathon a screenplay is like  usain bolt running the 100 meter sprint  at the olympics  there is there is no extraneous stuff in  a script and the idea for interceptors  suited a script because it would not  have had enough  depth of story to fill a 400 page book  but for a screenplay  it just rocketed along  and it was funny with interceptor that  we had a few offers  for people to buy the script uh but they  didn't want me to direct it and so so we  walked so the process is different um  and they're different arts you know  writing a novel is a  a far more  a long play long form  uh prospect whereas a screenplay is  short sharp fast get in get out make it  as exciting as possible did the  experience of directing live up to your  expectations  yes it was great  it's funny you know when you write a  novel  it's a it's a joy but it's a solitary  joy uh creating a story and when you  make a movie you get to work with all  these creative people and you get to  share  that creative joy  and that's that was just  wonderful so i i thoroughly enjoyed it i  was very very prepared i've been waiting  for about you know 36 years to do this  since i saw the making of return of the  jedi on tv so  i was ready to go  were there was there anything that you  found to be um  more or less difficult than what you  expected you know generally no uh if if  you're ready um  i mean you you have to be prepared to be  a director and you have to have all the  answers and i think  because i wrote the script  i had pictured the whole story in my  mind so anybody had a question what was  elsa's costume supposed to look like  what did the command center look like i  had all the answers can you talk a  little bit about the process of  writing for such a tiny set and did it  make things more difficult  or or easier because you were very  limited you know  keeping a story in a confined space is a  writing challenge  and  we used every square inch of that set  from the outer tunnels for the gunfight  for the unit tunnel for the first fight  that elsa has with a giant six foot two  guy  you have to keep it interesting and the  good thing about that command center is  it's just intrinsically interesting  it's got this map screen in the middle  it's got live  security camera footage those security  cameras were live while we were filming  it's got a direct line to the white  house it's got you know the submarine  being tracked it's got the televisions  so as long as you keep people interested  you can get away with having your story  in this single location as long as that  location is interesting if anything it  becomes a part of the story how much of  it was  based on fact versus how much of it was  stuff that you kind of guessed at or  made up the joy of setting your story in  highly classified places is that nobody  can prove you wrong  that's great  i mean who's gonna say oh no that's what  that's no no no that's what that's not  what sbx1 looks like there'd be  breaching classified information so  when you go into the classified stuff  the world is your oyster it's like the  hunt for in october it's fantastic  that said  the flight time for missiles is true 24  minutes and 12 minutes to shoot them  down uh spx-1 does exist fort greeley  exists on a on a more serious note  the sexual harassment subplot of the  lead jj  is based on  true  incidents that i looked up things like  bounty hunts on women who have you know  brought complaints in the military that  does happen  this has been something i've done in my  career as a novelist you  you tell a fun fast-paced entertaining  story  but you grounded in a solid reality and  that seems to work rather well it's  worked for me so far  can you talk a little bit about chris  hemsworth's cameo how did that um  get involved or how did  he come on to the screen uh chris picked  it chris chris had read the script uh  a long while ago with elsa and um and  obviously the producers of the movie are  saying hey you know chris be great if  you were you know in the movie  not judgement  and chris himself came back  and he said you know  i could play that dude in the uh the tv  store  and  and it was it was fantastic uh we  i he'd asked what the look was and i  said well this is the kind of look i'm  going for and um  with chris  uh chris is a really just genuine nice  guy  but once you get on the set  he is laser focused you don't mess  around  and so once he was on the set he was  like okay matt what do you want me to do  where do you want me to go what are we  doing here and i had that  that night we filmed we took over  a tv store in sydney it was like a best  buy in sydney  and we took it over  and i had every single moment for chris  laid out we left a lot of that on the  cutting room floor i have a whole lot  more  footage of chris hemsworth in  he's he's dynamite he's just he's he's  that good um and and we have we have  some photos of elsa she came to the set  that day and you see the two of them  together and they're just lovely  they're a really nice couple  well they're telling me to wrap up so um  thank you so much i really appreciate  you taking the time today you're very  welcome thank you very much  you
 

